+# CommitLore Distribution Strategies
+
+## Problem
+Half the team uses editors and agents that don't support the `onPreToolUse` hook. Those users get no context records at all. We cannot ship custom hooks for every tool on the market.
+
+## Solution: Three-layered approach
+
+### Layer 1: Pre-tool-use hook (existing)
+- **Who**: Claude Code (official), any editor with pre-tool-use hook support
+- **How**: Fires when the agent reads/writes a file; injects records scoped to that file
+- **Scope**: Perfectly targeted to one file at a time
+- **Latency**: ~ms per file (runs on critical path)
+- **Fallback**: None — if the hook fires, records arrive
+
+**No changes needed** — this layer continues to work as-is.
+
+### Layer 2: MCP Server (primary fallback)
+- **Who**: Claude Code, Claude API with MCP, any agent with MCP support
+- **How**: Exposes `commitlore_records` tool that agents can call to fetch records
+- **Scope**: On-demand; agents query for the file they're working on
+- **Latency**: Network roundtrip for MCP call (sub-100ms typically)
+- **Setup**: Add to `.claude/mcp.json` or agent MCP config
+
+**How it works**:
+1. Agent encounters a file it needs context for (Read, Edit, etc.)
+2. Agent calls `commitlore_records(repo, filePath)` via MCP
+3. MCP server returns formatted records for that file
+4. Agent includes records in its reasoning
+
+**Why this works**: MCP is a standard supported by Claude, most modern agents, and many editors. No per-tool hooks needed.
+
+### Layer 3: Proactive injection at conversation start
+- **Who**: Fallback for non-MCP systems; good for reducing latency on the most-common files
+- **How**: At session start, scans git for changed files and proactively injects their records
+- **Scope**: Limited to `maxFiles` (default 10) to avoid context explosion
+- **Latency**: One-time cost at session start; afterward, records are in context
+- **When to use**: Non-critical contexts, session-start setup, educational/review scenarios
+
+**How it works**:
+1. Conversation starts (via agent, prompt, or script)
+2. Scan for: modified files, staged changes, new files
+3. Fetch records for up to N files
+4. Inject into system context block
+5. Agent sees all context at once, no per-file lookup needed
+
+**Trade-offs**:
+- ✓ Works everywhere (no MCP/hook dependency)
+- ✓ No per-file latency
+- ✗ Scope is broader than one file (loses some targeting)
+- ✗ Paid only once per session, but cost is higher
+
+## Implementation guide
+
+### If you have hook support (Claude Code, etc.)
+- Layer 1 kicks in automatically. Records arrive when you touch files.
+
+### If you have MCP support
+- Configure the MCP server in your `.claude/mcp.json`:
+  ```json
+  {
+    "mcpServers": {
+      "commitlore": {
+        "command": "node",
+        "args": ["path/to/src/mcp/server.ts"]
+      }
+    }
+  }
+  ```
+- When you Read/Edit a file, your agent can call `commitlore_records` to fetch context.
+
+### If you have neither
+- Use proactive injection at session start:
+  ```typescript
+  import { proactivelyInjectRecords } from "./src/strategies/proactive-injection.ts";
+
+  const context = proactivelyInjectRecords({
+    repo: process.cwd(),
+    maxFiles: 10,
+  });
+  
+  // Inject `context` into your agent prompt
+  ```
+
+## Why this approach?
+
+**The core trade-off**: Scope vs. universality.
+- Layer 1 is maximally scoped and perfectly targeted, but requires per-tool hooks we can't maintain.
+- Layer 2 is universally available (MCP is standard) and on-demand (good scope), but agents must call the tool.
+- Layer 3 is universal but broadcasts to all files at once (loses some scope precision).
+
+We chose this three-layer model because:
+1. It preserves the hook-based high-precision injection for teams that have it.
+2. It provides a modern, standards-based fallback (MCP) for most teams.
+3. It includes a low-tech emergency escape hatch (proactive) for edge cases.
+
+The expected outcome: ~60% of teams use Layer 1 (hook) or Layer 2 (MCP), ~40% use Layer 3 as a last resort.
